{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018P8Yn2S8YoxFGjpCD6kMzd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The `error-invalid-format` test fails because GNU `gseq` outputs only the error line, but our Go implementation also outputs \"Try 'seq --help' for more information.\" after the error. Looking at the error output:\n\n- ref stderr: `seq: format '%d' has unknown %d directive\\n`\n- go stderr: `seq: format '%d' has unknown %d directive\\nTry 'seq --help' for more information.\\n`\n\nGNU seq doesn't print the \"Try 'seq --help'...\" line for format errors. Actually wait - let me check other error cases that pass. \n\nThe other error cases all pass because GNU gseq prints the help hint for those errors too, and the normalizer makes the binary names match. But for format errors specifically, GNU gseq omits that help hint entirely, so our `exitWithError` function needs to be more selective about when it appends that message.\n\nI need to figure out which error types in GNU seq actually get the \"Try 'seq --help'\" line and which don't, so I can replicate that behavior in the Go version.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1051,"cache_read_input_tokens":36810,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1051},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c605cc3-b7a7-4905-828b-3e90cba9af0d","uuid":"d0f2ad1f-1763-4f67-8390-9864e1c8e831"}
