{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Lms4noEaQkQJGDncw1QhCg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"So GNU seq only omits the \"Try 'seq --help'\" hint for the format directive error. The other errors (invalid floating point, missing operand, extra operand, format+equal-width conflict) all include the hint.\n\nLooking at the GNU source, for format errors like \"unknown %d directive\", it uses `error()` directly which doesn't print the hint. For other errors, it goes through a different code path.\n\nI need to modify `main.go` to not print the \"Try\" hint for format validation errors. The cleanest approach is to have `validateFormat` errors handled differently from `exitWithError`.\n\n\nNow I'm checking the exact GNU behavior for each error type to see which ones get the hint and which don't. It looks like format validation errors (unknown directive, missing directive, too many directives) are the only ones that skip the hint, while argument-related errors like missing operand, extra operand, and invalid floating point all include it. Let me verify the remaining edge cases like NaN and zero step arguments.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":793,"cache_read_input_tokens":37861,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":793},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c605cc3-b7a7-4905-828b-3e90cba9af0d","uuid":"717c99c8-3f99-434f-aeb4-db580827d1eb"}
